{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וישב", :translit "va-yeshev", :gloss "and he dwelt"} {:he "הסיר ה' משפטיך פנה אויביך", :translit "hesir Hashem mishpatayich panah oyveich", :gloss "YHWH has removed your judgments, has cleared your enemies"} {:he "וילך אל ארץ", :translit "va-yelech el eretz", :gloss "and he went to the land of"} {:he "וישב יעקב", :translit "va-yeshev Ya'akov", :gloss "and Jacob dwelt"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Contrast of verbs: Esau 'went' while Jacob 'dwelt,' each fulfilling a destiny."}], :frame {:text "Rashi cites a midrash that Jacob sought to dwell in tranquility after his years of labor, but the tribulations of Joseph sprang upon him. The verse from Zephaniah 3:15, 'YHWH has removed your judgments, has cleared your enemies,' is applied to Jacob: once Esau departed, the threat was cleared and Jacob could settle. The parable of winnowing separates Esau (straw and chaff, blown away) from Jacob (grain, remaining), echoing the oracle in Genesis 25:23 that the elder shall serve the younger.", :sources ["Genesis Rabbah 84:3" "Rashi on Genesis 37:2" "Zephaniah 3:15" "Genesis 25:23" "Genesis 36:6"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מגורי", :translit "megurei", :gloss "sojournings of"} {:he "מגורי אל חרב", :translit "megurei el cherev", :gloss "fear of the sword"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["מגורי" "מגורי אל חרב"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase מגורי אל חרב, 'fear of the sword,' appears in Job describing the wicked man's dread. The Ba'al HaTurim links it to Jacob's situation dwelling among potentially hostile neighbors, heightening the tension of 'and he dwelt': the dwelling was not tranquil but fearful. The parable of sitting among dogs illustrates Jacob's vulnerability among Esau and his chieftains.", :sources ["Job 15:21" "Genesis 36:15-19"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מגורי אביו", :translit "megurei aviv", :gloss "sojournings of his father"} {:he "חברון", :translit "chevron", :gloss "Hebron"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "מגורי אביו", :b "חברון"}], :frame {:text "The land of the sojournings of Isaac is identified with Hebron, since Genesis 35:27 states that Jacob came to Isaac his father at Mamre, which is Hebron. The gematria confirms the location of Jacob's dwelling.", :sources ["Genesis 35:27"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וישב יעקב בארץ מגורי אביו", :translit "va-yeshev Ya'akov be-eretz megurei aviv", :gloss "and Jacob dwelt in the land of the sojournings of his father"} {:he "וישב לקיים מצות כבוד", :translit "va-yashev le-kayem mitzvat kavod", :gloss "he dwelt to fulfill the commandment of honoring"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "וישב יעקב בארץ מגורי אביו", :b "וישב לקיים מצות כבוד"}], :frame {:text "The commandment to honor one's father is stated in Exodus 20:12. The Sifre on Deuteronomy 10:12 includes honoring parents among the commandments whose reward is extended across generations. Jacob's return to Isaac's land is read as fulfilling this obligation, and the reward principle extends to Esau's honor as well.", :sources ["Exodus 20:12" "Deuteronomy 5:16" "Sifre Deuteronomy 32"]}} {:quotes [{:he "מגורי", :translit "megurei", :gloss "sojournings of"} {:he "יו\"ד כפופה כמין כ\"ף", :translit "yud kefufah ke-min kaf", :gloss "yud bent in the shape of a kaf"} {:he "עזים מאתים", :translit "izim me'otayim", :gloss "two hundred goats"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The bent yud in מגורי symbolizes submission; the ten items Jacob sent to Esau correspond to ten blessings."}], :frame {:text "The ten blessings Isaac bestowed on Jacob are enumerated from Genesis 27:28-29 and Genesis 28:1-4. Jacob's gift to Esau in Genesis 32:14-16 is detailed with ten categories of livestock, beginning with 'two hundred goats.' The account in Genesis Rabbah 75:5 records that Jacob sent this gift to honor Esau in fulfillment of honoring his father Isaac, since Isaac had commanded Jacob regarding Esau. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the bent yud as symbolic of Jacob's humility.", :sources ["Genesis 27:28-29" "Genesis 28:1-4" "Genesis 32:14-16" "Genesis Rabbah 75:5"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Contrast of verbs: Esau 'went' while Jacob 'dwelt,' each fulfilling a destiny.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["מגורי" "מגורי אל חרב"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "מגורי", :refs ["Genesis 37:1" "Genesis 47:9" "Ezekiel 21:17" "Psalms 119:54" "Lamentations 2:22"], :n 5, :lens :exact} {:phrase "מגורי אל חרב", :refs ["Ezekiel 21:17"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :gematria, :a "מגורי אביו", :a-value 278, :b "חברון", :b-value 266, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "וישב יעקב בארץ מגורי אביו", :a-value 1071, :b "וישב לקיים מצות כבוד", :b-value 1076, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "The bent yud in מגורי symbolizes submission; the ten items Jacob sent to Esau correspond to ten blessings.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:10:28.343553339Z", :model "glm-5.2"}